WEBVTT

07:22.360 --> 07:31.080
Good morning and welcome to

07:31.800 --> 07:34.920
Community and Health Services Committee meeting for Wednesday or Tuesday,

07:35.420 --> 07:39.293
March the 10th. I'd like to now

07:39.793 --> 07:43.040
call on Tim Brubaker, Regional Clerk to conduct the roll call. Thank you.

07:43.540 --> 07:46.920
Regional Chair Redman. Present. Councillor Craig. Chair Erb.

07:47.480 --> 07:51.230
Present. Councillor Foxton. Present. Councillor Gowing. Councillor Harris,

07:51.310 --> 07:54.590
present. Councillor Hunik,

07:57.710 --> 08:01.070
Councillor James, present. Councillor Leggett, present. Councillor McCabe, present.

08:01.150 --> 08:04.190
I heard Councillor McCabe, Councillor Nowak,

08:04.670 --> 08:08.350
Councillor Rodriguez, present. Councillor Salinon, Councillor Shawn, present. Councillor Rubanovich,

08:08.850 --> 08:11.550
Councillor Wolfe, thank you, chair. Thank you.

08:12.350 --> 08:15.790
As we begin this meeting, we are again reminded that many of our homes

08:16.290 --> 08:20.700
and places of employment in Waterloo region are situated on

08:21.340 --> 08:24.580
the land that is the traditional territory

08:25.080 --> 08:27.866
of the Haudenosaunee, Anishinaabe and Chinatown peoples. We recognise and deeply appreciate their historic connection

08:28.366 --> 08:31.940
to Waterloo Region and we also recognise the contributions Indigenous

08:32.440 --> 08:36.780
peoples have made in shaping and strengthening this community. We are grateful

08:37.280 --> 08:39.486
for the opportunity to live and work here and reaffirm our collective commitment to keep

08:39.986 --> 08:44.100
the promise that reconciliation takes place through learning and

08:44.600 --> 08:49.520
community building with Indigenous people who continue to live here.

08:50.020 --> 08:52.440
We are all treaty people with a responsibility to honour all of our relations.

08:53.080 --> 08:56.520
Are there any conflicts of interest under the Miscellaneous Conflict of

08:57.020 --> 09:01.240
Interest Act? None. We have no presentations. We have no delegations.

09:01.740 --> 09:05.080
We can move on to agenda item 6.1, strategic priority

09:05.580 --> 09:08.760
homes for all.

09:11.320 --> 09:14.560
I'd like to invite Julie Wheeler, Director of Senior

09:15.060 --> 09:19.210
Services to the podium to introduce our report.

09:19.710 --> 09:23.450
Thank you and good morning members of the Health and Community Services Committee.

09:24.410 --> 09:28.330
My name is Julie Wheeler. I'm the Director of Senior

09:28.830 --> 09:32.490
Services. The Senior Services Division works to support adults and older adults to

09:32.730 --> 09:34.330
age in our community with dignity, ensuring that there are spaces and places where people

09:34.830 --> 09:38.050
feel listened to and cared about. Here today, I'm so pleased to be

09:38.550 --> 09:41.930
introducing you to Dr. Fred Mather

09:42.430 --> 09:45.930
who has been our Medical Director for over 30

09:47.010 --> 09:50.290
30 years and has worked in the long-term care sector for over 40 years.

09:50.790 --> 09:54.010
Since we were last here, Dr. Mather was the recipient of the

09:54.510 --> 09:57.890
King Charles Coronation Medal in recognition of his tremendous contribution

09:57.970 --> 10:01.010
to the long-term care sector

10:01.650 --> 10:05.290
here in our community, in the province and across Canada.

10:05.790 --> 10:09.010
And we have the great joy and

10:09.510 --> 10:13.103
privilege of having Dr. Mather as

10:13.603 --> 10:17.040
the medical director for the region the regional home,

10:17.540 --> 10:20.720
Sunnyside Home. And so we're really pleased to have

10:21.600 --> 10:26.000
Dr. Mather here this morning to share

10:26.560 --> 10:30.160
with you a report on how things are going at Sunnyside Home in the

10:30.660 --> 10:33.786
last year. Welcome, Dr. Mather. Thank you, Julie. And it's my pleasure to return here

10:34.286 --> 10:38.560
and give an update on what's happening at Sunnyside Home. And I

10:38.800 --> 10:42.560
will conclude with some reflections about long-term care

10:42.720 --> 10:47.520
in Ontario in general.

10:47.820 --> 10:51.460
I'll touch on these things briefly. Changes in the data analytics,

10:51.960 --> 10:56.300
the collection and reporting of quality data in long-term care,

10:56.800 --> 11:00.300
the exciting living classroom that

11:00.800 --> 11:04.540
we have at Sunnyside Home for

11:05.040 --> 11:09.380
training personal support workers, research project which is just getting

11:09.880 --> 11:13.340
underway on trauma-informed care in long-term care, and an update on

11:13.840 --> 11:14.860
advanced care planning.

11:16.140 --> 11:19.260
And consent. Next slide.

11:19.740 --> 11:24.620
The vision is a caring community where everyone can live

11:25.120 --> 11:28.780
their best lives. Next slide. The vision is achieved

11:29.100 --> 11:32.620
through providing the highest quality

11:33.120 --> 11:36.260
of care and service,

11:36.760 --> 11:40.460
creating an inclusive, thriving, and welcoming community and inspiring

11:40.960 --> 11:45.020
people to live

11:45.520 --> 11:49.270
hopeful, happy and

11:49.770 --> 11:53.510
healthy lives. Next slide. Sunnyside provides a spectrum of

11:54.010 --> 11:58.070
care. We have the housing for

11:58.570 --> 12:01.470
seniors, affordable housing,

12:03.070 --> 12:06.510
supportive housing, long-term care, and community programs.

12:07.010 --> 12:10.510
And I would particularly emphasize our community Alzheimer program,

12:11.010 --> 12:14.470
which is very well used and associated with that

12:14.630 --> 12:18.190
is respite care where the same individuals that

12:18.690 --> 12:22.710
may come for the daycare programs can also have

12:23.210 --> 12:27.430
respite care where a caregiver may be on holidays

12:27.930 --> 12:31.590
for a week or two or just needs a break.

12:32.150 --> 12:35.870
It's the same staff that's providing

12:36.370 --> 12:40.430
their care in respite care as they are in the

12:40.930 --> 12:44.450
day program. Next slide.

12:44.950 --> 12:49.330
So just a bit

12:49.830 --> 12:54.330
on data analytics and long-term care. Long-term care in Ontario

12:54.490 --> 12:58.130
is heavily regulated,

12:58.630 --> 13:02.690
as I'm sure you all know. Long-term care

13:03.190 --> 13:06.530
sits at the intersection of healthcare, housing, and human rights, caring for a population that

13:07.030 --> 13:10.290
is both medically complex and structurally vulnerable. In the past year, the collection of

13:10.790 --> 13:14.290
data in the interRAI system has undergone a change to

13:14.790 --> 13:17.930
interRAI long-term care, which gives us more timely and specific data.

13:18.430 --> 13:21.730
As an aside, an international researcher and leader

13:22.230 --> 13:26.090
in the interRAI system is Dr. John Hirdes at the University of Waterloo, and he

13:26.590 --> 13:30.330
was recognised with the Order of Canada for

13:30.830 --> 13:34.410
his work here a couple of years ago.

13:35.610 --> 13:39.370
So the other change

13:39.450 --> 13:42.650
too is how our quality

13:42.810 --> 13:46.750
indicators are reported. And they're reported at the Canadian Institute

13:47.250 --> 13:50.550
for Health Information site, which is accessible to all the

13:51.050 --> 13:54.630
public and to compare the national,

13:55.130 --> 13:58.790
the Canadian quality indicators with the

13:59.290 --> 14:03.430
province and also with individual homes. Next slide.

14:03.930 --> 14:07.950
I wanted to just highlight the two quality

14:08.450 --> 14:11.430
indicators that are encircled in red there.

14:13.090 --> 14:17.570
The one on the far right is the use of physical restraints in

14:18.070 --> 14:21.250
long-term care. And the number for Sunnyside is 1.7%, which if you recall

14:21.750 --> 14:27.236
the previous slide, it was 1.4 for the province. So we're at much the

14:27.736 --> 14:31.330
same level. But this is something we have addressed because when we looked at

14:31.810 --> 14:35.890
it a few years ago, by having comparative

14:36.210 --> 14:40.170
quality indicators, we were more around six to 7%,

14:40.670 --> 14:44.340
which we felt was unacceptable. And we've

14:44.840 --> 14:48.420
had a quality improvement program to bring it down to the

14:48.920 --> 14:53.260
current level. We've also just finished a similar effort to minimize,

14:53.760 --> 14:58.060
almost eliminate, the use of

14:58.540 --> 15:02.540
bed rails at Sunnyside Home. Although very common in the hospital environment,

15:03.040 --> 15:05.380
physical restraints and bed rails are frowned upon in long-term care, not only because they

15:05.880 --> 15:09.870
restrict freedom and mobility, but the risks then outweigh any possible benefits

15:10.370 --> 15:14.070
by being a potential cause of serious injury.

15:14.570 --> 15:16.310
The other data I've highlighted there is our potentially inappropriate use of antipsychotic medication,

15:16.810 --> 15:20.870
which is 14.9%. On the previous slide,

15:22.390 --> 15:25.990
which was the Ontario number,

15:26.790 --> 15:30.710
it's more around 20%. It's something

15:31.210 --> 15:34.770
we've managed to keep low and much lower than the local,

15:35.270 --> 15:39.010
provincial and national levels. And it's one thing that the media

15:39.510 --> 15:43.770
in particularly looks

15:44.270 --> 15:48.130
at when they're looking at care

15:48.630 --> 15:51.330
in long-term care homes.

15:51.890 --> 15:55.490
Next slide. So the Living

15:56.050 --> 16:00.090
Classroom is an

16:00.590 --> 16:03.490
innovative educational partnership between the region of Waterloo, St.

16:03.810 --> 16:07.670
Louis Adult Learning Centre and continuing education centres, and is a dedicated space

16:08.170 --> 16:14.190
where public support workers are trained

16:14.350 --> 16:19.350
through hands-on learning, mentorship, and deeper engagement with residents.

16:19.850 --> 16:23.670
The initiative is part of a broader province-wide trend to

16:24.170 --> 16:28.470
integrate education and long-term care homes

16:28.970 --> 16:32.670
to better prepare health care workers. Next slide. This is

16:33.170 --> 16:36.510
a group of the initial group of students who were, there were 16 all

16:36.750 --> 16:40.650
together that graduated. And they gave overwhelming positive reviews of the

16:41.150 --> 16:45.010
program. Next slide. And it was a very positive experience for them looking at the

16:45.510 --> 16:50.810
feedback we received. Next slide. And this is

16:51.210 --> 16:54.810
the next group of graduates. Personal support workers are very

16:54.970 --> 16:58.570
important members of the healthcare team. In fact, I would say they're the most important

16:58.810 --> 17:02.650
ones because they're the ones that give the most attention

17:03.150 --> 17:06.910
to the day-to-day care of the residents, their training is very

17:07.410 --> 17:11.190
important. Since the Fixing Long-Term

17:11.910 --> 17:15.590
Care Homes Act was passed in 2021,

17:16.090 --> 17:19.670
there's that requirement that every resident gets some four hours

17:20.070 --> 17:23.990
of personal care. So there's been a real demand

17:24.490 --> 17:26.950
to increase the pool of PSWs there. And this is how we can contribute to

17:27.450 --> 17:31.110
meeting that increasing demand. Next slide. And research project that is just getting underway.

17:31.610 --> 17:35.190
It's a four-year research project and we which we have received funding through the Canadian

17:35.750 --> 17:39.390
Institute of Health Research and the Canadian

17:39.890 --> 17:43.310
Consortium on Neurodegeneration and Aging is Trauma Informed Care. Trauma Informed Care

17:43.810 --> 17:47.790
in long-term care setting is approach that recognises the widespread impact of

17:48.290 --> 17:51.830
trauma among older adults and seeks to create care environments

17:51.910 --> 17:54.310
that promote safety, respect,

17:56.630 --> 17:57.910
choice, and empowerment.

18:00.550 --> 18:04.230
While actively working to prevent retraumatization. Many older

18:04.730 --> 18:07.910
adults experience traumatic events over their lifetimes which affect

18:08.310 --> 18:11.830
their responses to

18:12.330 --> 18:16.509
care and the reenactment of trauma

18:17.009 --> 18:20.790
when they may enter an institutional setting. The chief investigator

18:21.350 --> 18:25.590
of this is with Dr. Sophia Benjamin

18:26.090 --> 18:29.990
who's a geriatric psychiatrist here. It is what's called

18:30.070 --> 18:33.750
an open design type research project where the initial

18:34.630 --> 18:38.710
phase, which will get underway

18:39.210 --> 18:42.870
this year, is interviewing residents, substitute decision makers, staff,

18:43.370 --> 18:47.230
and management about what their understanding is

18:47.730 --> 18:51.110
of the impact of trauma in the

18:51.610 --> 18:55.110
residents that we look after.

18:58.360 --> 19:02.040
And then the next step will be

19:02.540 --> 19:05.920
to look at ways of increasing awareness of the

19:06.420 --> 19:10.160
care staff in dealing with trauma-informed care. Next slide.

19:10.660 --> 19:14.160
I just wanted one thing

19:14.660 --> 19:18.280
that we need to provide ongoing education, leadership and

19:18.440 --> 19:21.800
action is the whole topic of advanced care

19:22.300 --> 19:24.960
planning. And consent.

19:25.440 --> 19:28.960
This is something that the region of

19:29.360 --> 19:32.480
Waterloo was a real leader in in the 1990s.

19:32.980 --> 19:37.080
Some of you might recall these forms that were used in the emergency

19:37.580 --> 19:41.520
departments in hospital, in home care, in family physicians

19:41.600 --> 19:45.200
office, and in long-term care to provide means of making decisions as

19:45.440 --> 19:46.560
to what you would like for your care or the person that you're the substitute

19:47.060 --> 19:50.920
decision maker for if they're approaching end of life or have a

19:51.320 --> 19:55.520
significant change in their condition. Next slide. Now, these forms,

19:56.020 --> 20:00.120
unfortunately, although they were widely used and people would have

20:00.760 --> 20:04.280
them on their refrigerator, for example,

20:04.780 --> 20:07.960
just in case they had to go to the emergency

20:08.200 --> 20:12.080
department and they had their choices for aggressive or less aggressive care

20:12.580 --> 20:15.760
clearly stated. However, with the passage of the Substitute Decisions Act

20:16.260 --> 20:21.250
and the Health Care Consent Act, they were considered not

20:21.750 --> 20:25.330
appropriate forms because they implied consent. And according to the

20:25.830 --> 20:29.450
Health Care Consent Act, consent needs to be

20:29.950 --> 20:33.650
always time specific and decision specific. So we've gone from

20:34.130 --> 20:38.050
talking about levels of

20:38.550 --> 20:41.570
care or advanced directives to advanced care planning, goals of care,

20:42.070 --> 20:45.570
and whatever is provided in terms of care

20:47.510 --> 20:51.510
requires consent, again, which is both time

20:53.110 --> 20:55.110
specific and decision specific.

20:55.190 --> 20:59.790
So we are underway in

21:00.290 --> 21:03.190
2026 to review our own policy on advanced care planning.

21:03.510 --> 21:07.830
Next slide. And we created

21:09.670 --> 21:13.910
this a few years back and it's due for some updates.

21:14.310 --> 21:17.710
Our policy is consent is

21:18.210 --> 21:22.290
a conversation. Where would I like

21:22.790 --> 21:26.770
to be cared for when my condition changes?

21:26.930 --> 21:45.090
I'd like to

21:45.590 --> 21:51.240
just, Thank Julie for her introduction. I was honoured on

21:51.740 --> 21:55.520
March 29th last year to receive the King Charles III

21:56.020 --> 21:58.760
Coronation Medal for my work in long-term care.

21:59.260 --> 22:02.520
It was presented to me by the Minister, Dr.

22:03.020 --> 22:07.240
Natalia GuscinDova-Bashta. I was also honoured in October to give the keynote

22:07.740 --> 22:11.240
address at the Ontario Long-Term Care Clinicians Annual Convention. I was

22:11.740 --> 22:15.660
asked to give the history of the Ontario Long-Term

22:16.160 --> 22:19.633
Care Physicians, which was a charitable

22:20.133 --> 22:24.260
organisation which became in 2016 the Ontario Long-Term Care Clinicians

22:24.760 --> 22:28.460
under my leadership, a not-for-profit which now provides a lot

22:29.980 --> 22:33.500
of advocacy and education in particular

22:34.000 --> 22:37.820
the medical director course that is mandated in

22:38.320 --> 22:41.590
the current act. Throughout my preparation and the

22:41.670 --> 22:45.750
feedback I've received since then, I came to

22:46.250 --> 22:49.990
the revelation how significant the

22:51.110 --> 22:54.390
decade of the 1990s was for long-term care. We saw it particularly

22:54.890 --> 22:58.790
here in Waterloo region where there was the closure of chronic care

22:59.290 --> 23:03.150
hospitals. Freeport Hospital at that time had

23:03.650 --> 23:06.550
over 300 chronic care beds which were closed throughout that decade.

23:07.870 --> 23:08.030
Decade,

23:08.530 --> 23:11.870
many of those patients

23:12.370 --> 23:16.350
in Freeport became

23:17.070 --> 23:21.590
residents in chronic care with a higher acuity of problems with

23:22.090 --> 23:25.230
catheters, feeding tubes, and complex wounds. We also saw the

23:25.630 --> 23:30.190
repatriation of long-term

23:30.690 --> 23:34.190
mental illness patients from the geriatric psychiatric units at St. Thomas and London

23:34.690 --> 23:38.520
Psychiatric hospital coming back into long-term care. And there was that

23:39.020 --> 23:42.440
ongoing process of the closure of the old Ontario hospital

23:43.320 --> 23:47.000
schools, the big ones being in Blenheim, Orillia, and Smith Falls. And a

23:47.500 --> 23:51.360
lot of those people, though most of them

23:51.860 --> 23:55.320
actually went into community living,

23:55.820 --> 24:00.480
which was very good, some had complex medical needs and

24:00.980 --> 24:05.000
went into long-term care. We also saw a marked expansion of long-term care. And long-term

24:05.500 --> 24:09.640
care became very, very restrained. And it took

24:10.140 --> 24:13.480
another 20 years and the COVID pandemic to realise

24:13.980 --> 24:17.600
how strained staffing and resources were in

24:18.100 --> 24:21.480
long-term care, which has led to,

24:21.980 --> 24:25.200
I think, a turnaround in the last few years as we've seen the changes with

24:25.700 --> 24:29.440
the Fixing Long-Term Care Homes Act. One other change that I just mentioned,

24:29.940 --> 24:32.380
and this is why this body and the region are so important in long-term care

24:32.880 --> 24:33.840
is that back in the 1990s, almost half of long-term care homes in Ontario,

24:34.340 --> 24:38.080
which included at that time homes for the age, which would have been the

24:38.580 --> 24:42.480
term used for Sunnyside, were operated by the public, by municipalities.

24:42.560 --> 24:46.320
And then there became a large degree of private

24:46.720 --> 24:51.040
ownership of long-term care. And now municipal

24:51.540 --> 24:55.520
homes account for 16% of the

24:56.080 --> 24:56.880
homes.

24:57.380 --> 25:01.620
And there

25:01.940 --> 25:05.620
hasn't been any increase in municipal homes,

25:05.860 --> 25:08.860
although they are considered the exemplary homes. And I'm pleased to be working with medical

25:09.360 --> 25:13.860
directors of the two homes in York region, the four or five in Toronto,

25:16.340 --> 25:20.020
Wellington Terrace and Fergus, as homes that will

25:20.520 --> 25:23.860
provide examples of quality care in long-term care.

25:24.390 --> 25:27.750
So thank you for

25:27.910 --> 25:31.110
letting me give you this update. It's been

25:32.310 --> 25:35.590
my, it's been important for me to share all

25:36.090 --> 25:39.110
this with you. Thanks, Dr. Mather, don't go far. We have some questions for you.

25:40.550 --> 25:43.670
First, Councillor Hunic. Thank you,

25:43.910 --> 25:47.590
Chair Herb, and thank you, Dr. Mather, for your work

25:48.090 --> 25:48.750
in our community and our regional home, as well as your great report today.

25:49.250 --> 25:52.990
I'm particularly passionate about the reduction of physical restraints and

25:53.490 --> 25:56.510
Sunny side being noted for that.

25:57.010 --> 26:04.030
Thank you for that work. I'm also passionate

26:04.530 --> 26:08.550
about ensuring that safety standards are the same across regional services, including Mobility

26:09.050 --> 26:12.710
Plus. Could you clarify?

26:13.210 --> 26:16.550
According to the Ministry of Long-Term Care,

26:18.740 --> 26:22.380
care and therefore Sunnyside home as

26:22.880 --> 26:27.300
well. Could you clarify are lap belts on

26:27.460 --> 26:30.820
wheelchairs considered a physical restraint? That's a

26:31.320 --> 26:35.500
very good question and it's a matter of interpretation. If the resident can release the

26:36.000 --> 26:39.940
lap belt, it's not a physical restraint. If they

26:40.440 --> 26:42.900
can't release it, it's considered a physical restraint.

26:43.530 --> 26:46.770
There is some nuance there and this explains some of the variation in the data

26:47.270 --> 26:50.930
though. It requires a critical review in some cases because there's

26:51.430 --> 26:55.610
also the personal assistant safety device where

26:56.110 --> 27:00.970
it's done more for reasons of positioning or

27:01.470 --> 27:04.690
comfort. Long-term care homes get a lot of

27:05.190 --> 27:09.250
inspections and that's something that the inspectors

27:09.750 --> 27:13.140
look at. It may be classified as a P-A-S-D, but in reality,

27:13.640 --> 27:18.753
it may be a restraint. So there is, I'll just say

27:19.253 --> 27:21.140
it again, there's a bit of a nuance there,

27:22.580 --> 27:26.660
how that's interpreted. But anything that a resident

27:27.160 --> 27:31.140
can't free themselves of would be considered,

27:31.640 --> 27:37.260
strictly speaking, a physical restraint. Thank you. Your wisdom will be noted for a

27:37.760 --> 27:41.080
Mobility Plus discussion later today. Appreciate it.

27:41.580 --> 27:44.600
Take care. Okay, thank you.

27:45.100 --> 27:48.720
Councillor Cowan. Thanks, Chair. Thanks for bringing this in. I always

27:49.220 --> 27:52.640
love visiting Sunnyside and especially

27:53.140 --> 27:56.199
the new Alzheimer Society, sorry,

27:56.280 --> 27:59.760
chapter that you have there. One of the slides that you

28:00.260 --> 28:03.480
had there, it mentioned personal, try that again,

28:03.980 --> 28:09.670
potentially inappropriate use of anti- psychotics in

28:10.170 --> 28:14.790
long-term care. The title

28:15.290 --> 28:18.550
of that, to me, struck me when I read that. Can you explain it a

28:19.050 --> 28:20.790
little more? Because, like, potentially inappropriate, like that just.

28:22.550 --> 28:26.550
Thank you for the question. What is appropriate

28:26.630 --> 28:29.870
use? There's about five indications for

28:30.370 --> 28:33.270
appropriate use. Schizophrenia, Huntington's dementia. Hallucinations,

28:33.770 --> 28:37.150
delusions, and end of care,

28:37.650 --> 28:40.990
where they're used mainly for

28:41.490 --> 28:45.390
off-label reasons at that time. Those are

28:45.890 --> 28:49.430
the appropriate ones. The potentially inappropriate ones are the

28:49.930 --> 28:53.830
ones that don't fall into those five definitions. But they

28:54.330 --> 28:57.750
may include what would be medically considered

28:58.250 --> 29:02.190
very appropriate. For example, for major

29:02.690 --> 29:06.050
depression and bipolar illness, antipsychotics may be either first or

29:06.550 --> 29:10.570
second line treatments for them. But what is particularly focused

29:11.070 --> 29:14.610
on in long-term care, that is

29:15.110 --> 29:19.170
they're not abused, is when it's used

29:19.670 --> 29:23.890
for aggressive behaviours in dementia, like Alzheimer's

29:24.390 --> 29:28.210
disease. And they may have

29:28.710 --> 29:32.210
a role there, but it's felt to be a very limited role.

29:34.000 --> 29:37.600
And that's what's especially looked

29:38.100 --> 29:42.120
at with that quality

29:42.620 --> 29:45.680
indicator. Okay, thank you. That clears it up. Councillor Leggett.

29:46.320 --> 29:49.840
Thank you, Mr. Chair. I have a couple, but one was connected

29:50.340 --> 29:54.480
to Councillor Gowing

29:54.800 --> 30:00.230
had asked, so if Antipsychotic

30:00.730 --> 30:04.630
drugs are, you're trying not to use them and also

30:05.130 --> 30:07.070
restraints are trying not to be used. How are you taking care, making sure people

30:07.570 --> 30:09.790
are safe? Like if somebody gets up in the middle of the night to use

30:10.290 --> 30:13.750
the washroom, I mean we all have, I heard those stories.

30:14.390 --> 30:18.470
So we have relatives who've got up to go to the bathroom unassisted

30:18.550 --> 30:22.350
because they feel they should be able to do that and then they

30:22.850 --> 30:26.500
fall and break their hip. So How are you dealing with

30:27.000 --> 30:28.420
that one? And then if you don't mind, I'd like to do my follow-up question

30:29.140 --> 30:33.140
after that. Sorry, my other question after that. The real emphasis is on non-pharmacological

30:33.220 --> 30:37.300
interventions. So for the person who's getting up at night,

30:37.540 --> 30:41.180
and they may be very confused and very high risk

30:41.680 --> 30:45.860
for a fall, they would have a bed alarm. So when their

30:46.340 --> 30:51.460
weight shifts off the bed, an alarm goes off

30:51.860 --> 30:55.640
and a PSW or a nurse on the floor would come

30:55.960 --> 30:59.800
to assist that person to go to the washroom.

30:59.880 --> 31:03.040
In terms of bed rails, which can cause very

31:03.540 --> 31:06.360
serious injuries in the cognitively impaired person,

31:06.680 --> 31:10.560
having a low

31:11.060 --> 31:14.840
bed where there's

31:15.340 --> 31:18.920
mats beside the bed, so if they were to slide off. So sometimes where

31:19.420 --> 31:23.620
they're needed to help with transfers in bed, like in a partial paralytic patient,

31:23.780 --> 31:27.260
using like a fireman's pole beside the

31:27.760 --> 31:31.580
bed, is a safer technique

31:32.080 --> 31:36.060
than having a bed rail up. May I ask my next question? I'm particularly interested

31:36.560 --> 31:41.060
in the living classroom as well as the research

31:41.220 --> 31:44.420
in trauma-informed care. We're hearing more and more that our long-term

31:44.920 --> 31:50.020
care facilities are taking in patients

31:50.520 --> 31:54.300
or clients who have mental health issues or addicts and that there's problems within

31:55.100 --> 31:58.220
those places for the elderly who don't have those problems.

31:58.720 --> 32:02.220
In particular in BC, I hear it's been going on

32:04.700 --> 32:07.940
for quite a while and I know it's

32:08.440 --> 32:12.060
happening in Ontario now. The living classroom, if you're dealing with

32:12.560 --> 32:13.540
PSWs and their training, are they being trained in that themselves and de-escalation? How does

32:14.040 --> 32:18.980
that work? And could you speak to the research in

32:19.480 --> 32:23.540
trauma-informed care and how that is applied there? Thank you. So the first part of

32:24.040 --> 32:25.460
your question is the train for the PSWs. I can't really go into all their

32:25.960 --> 32:28.420
curriculum because I'm not directly involved with it, but yes, it does deal with the

32:28.920 --> 32:36.420
spectrum of mental illness in long-term care, people with serious

32:36.920 --> 32:39.940
mental illness, people with schizophrenia, bipolar disease, schizoaffective disease, and long-term there

32:40.440 --> 32:44.930
that they can recognise them and

32:45.430 --> 32:48.610
the behaviours that may be associated with that.

32:49.170 --> 32:52.370
And also probably a

32:52.870 --> 32:56.770
much larger group of challenging residents are people with

32:57.010 --> 33:01.810
more advanced

33:02.310 --> 33:05.890
dementia where they have inappropriate behaviours, aggressive behaviours, loss of frontal lobe

33:06.130 --> 33:08.850
inhibition where they can make inappropriate comments of a sexual or racist nature. That's all

33:09.350 --> 33:13.590
part of the training. The research

33:14.090 --> 33:23.910
in

33:24.070 --> 33:27.750
trauma-informed care is looking at probably

33:28.250 --> 33:32.070
a wide range of trauma and that's part of the phase

33:32.230 --> 33:35.990
one. When we think of past trauma,

33:36.310 --> 33:39.350
we think of the effects of war, displacement, abuse, physical, sexual, emotional.

33:40.000 --> 33:43.520
But also there's other trauma. Part of this

33:43.680 --> 33:48.240
that I'm particularly interested in and I think

33:48.740 --> 33:52.560
we can look at in

33:53.060 --> 33:56.400
this project is the effects of the trauma of the COVID-19

33:56.900 --> 34:00.440
pandemic because I think that took a big toll

34:00.940 --> 34:04.080
on people, the isolation, the lack of visitors during that time.

34:04.640 --> 34:08.440
And if we were to have another major pandemic like that,

34:08.940 --> 34:12.240
we might want to reconsider the severity

34:12.740 --> 34:17.000
of the restrictions of individuals living

34:17.500 --> 34:20.960
in long-term care because I think

34:21.460 --> 34:25.480
there's sort of an untold toll that that took on

34:25.980 --> 34:29.160
individuals in long-term care. The research project itself is a

34:29.660 --> 34:34.480
matter of interviews, getting the attitudes and

34:34.980 --> 34:41.450
the opinions of staff, residents, caregivers, and then developing tools for

34:41.950 --> 34:45.370
education and assessment of residents in identifying the

34:45.450 --> 34:48.890
impact of past trauma into

34:49.130 --> 34:52.970
their current health situation and also the

34:54.330 --> 34:58.330
impact of institutionalization. To go back to studies that were well

34:58.650 --> 35:02.490
published with the works of Dr.

35:02.810 --> 35:05.850
Michael Gordon at Baycrest, for example,

35:06.300 --> 35:10.500
because for decades he

35:11.000 --> 35:14.540
worked with Holocaust survivors at Baycrest

35:15.040 --> 35:19.260
in Toronto. And his work identified the

35:19.760 --> 35:24.020
impact of their experiences in concentration camps

35:24.520 --> 35:28.380
and post-war diaspora on their experience

35:28.880 --> 35:32.100
of going into an institution than later in

35:32.600 --> 35:38.100
life. That's a very

35:38.600 --> 35:41.820
dramatic example, but that's the type of information we

35:42.320 --> 35:45.740
want to get or look for the information from an

35:46.240 --> 35:50.140
individual's past that we might be missing right now. So to

35:50.620 --> 35:54.620
deal with the part of my question that looked

35:54.860 --> 35:58.100
at those who had addiction issues where

35:58.600 --> 36:02.860
it's affected their brains and they're much younger, age group,

36:03.360 --> 36:06.940
but they have the strength of a much younger age group

36:07.440 --> 36:12.140
and it puts at risk those

36:12.640 --> 36:16.180
in these facilities. How are you, is that part of your research in trauma-informed

36:16.260 --> 36:20.580
care or how are the PSWs

36:21.460 --> 36:25.060
being trained to handle that? I'm not sure if the particular

36:25.560 --> 36:30.600
resident you're referring to would be

36:31.000 --> 36:35.240
best served in long-term care. They may be

36:35.640 --> 36:39.000
better off in specialized mental health or in complex continuing care,

36:42.440 --> 36:44.960
the types of programs we have at the Wren Chickapee site right now. Every resident

36:45.460 --> 36:49.000
has a care plan and a care plan applies to the

36:49.500 --> 36:53.400
whole members of the team and particularly

36:53.900 --> 36:57.920
the PSW. And resident

36:59.040 --> 37:02.840
on staff injury is a very important

37:03.340 --> 37:06.920
thing. So it would be part of the care plan and the

37:07.420 --> 37:10.933
PSW would be trained on knowing what the care plan

37:11.433 --> 37:15.680
is and knowing what protection they need to take.

37:15.920 --> 37:19.240
So we don't have any of those types of patients in Sunnyside? I'm asking because

37:19.740 --> 37:22.560
I know some long-term care facilities are taking more and more because there's no place

37:23.060 --> 37:26.730
for them to go. Yeah. We do have the type of resident

37:27.230 --> 37:30.650
that you described, yes. Okay, thank you. Yeah. Chair Redmond. Thank you,

37:31.150 --> 37:35.370
Chair Redmond. Dr. Mayers, you're like the most

37:35.870 --> 37:39.330
interesting professor that I ever had in university. You're as

37:39.830 --> 37:43.250
perennial as the seasons and I want to

37:43.650 --> 37:46.876
thank you so much for your dedication and your wisdom and

37:47.376 --> 37:49.010
your leadership in the health sector.

37:51.480 --> 37:54.920
I've had some really interesting conversations about AI and how it relates to long-term

37:55.420 --> 37:58.720
care and specifically to Sunnyside. And I think it was Trinity where

37:59.220 --> 38:02.504
I first was introduced to smart toilets, but I was also

38:03.004 --> 38:05.680
told that Sunnyside used AI as one of the ways to augment patient care.

38:06.180 --> 38:09.400
So I don't know if this is a question for you, Dr. Mather, or for

38:09.900 --> 38:11.000
Julie, but can you talk a little bit about how AI is enhancing how we

38:11.320 --> 38:16.640
care for the residents sunny

38:17.140 --> 38:18.960
side? I will pass that off to Julie.

38:19.460 --> 38:22.680
It's a very good, good question

38:23.180 --> 38:27.800
because AI has transformed medical care in

38:28.300 --> 38:32.280
the last couple of years. We saw the

38:32.780 --> 38:36.800
evolution from textbooks to more online learning, and now

38:37.300 --> 38:41.240
it's generative AI and

38:41.740 --> 38:45.120
good resources. It's changing medical care. It's changing

38:45.620 --> 38:49.020
medical education greatly. From a personal point of

38:49.520 --> 38:53.380
view, I'm part of most physicians now who are using AI scribe

38:53.880 --> 38:57.420
to some degree that helps to

38:57.920 --> 39:01.140
organise your observations, your physical findings,

39:01.220 --> 39:04.820
your investigative results, which makes for much better

39:05.320 --> 39:11.380
record keeping overall, recognising at the same time all the pitfalls that

39:12.100 --> 39:15.930
generative AI in may

39:16.430 --> 39:20.050
have. Julie? Yes, through you, the chair. Thank you for the question. Full honesty,

39:20.550 --> 39:24.650
I think when it comes to AI, we are still much further

39:25.150 --> 39:31.170
behind than what I think the opportunity is for us.

39:31.670 --> 39:35.490
We have been involved in some work

39:35.990 --> 39:39.450
using robotic companions for a tool for engagement, which speaking,

39:40.010 --> 39:43.920
going back to what Dr. Mather said earlier, We are very motivated to

39:44.420 --> 39:48.680
overcome the isolation and loneliness that

39:49.180 --> 39:53.280
people feel in long-term care. And I think the pandemic

39:53.780 --> 39:57.080
put a burning platform underneath us to try to figure out ways to help people

39:57.580 --> 40:01.280
engage no matter what their circumstances are.

40:01.780 --> 40:04.720
And so we do see that technology has that option. And in particular, AI,

40:05.220 --> 40:09.480
where we can have an access resources for people now to

40:09.980 --> 40:13.170
engage with the world outside of Sunnyside and even

40:13.670 --> 40:16.930
within. And so very early in our process, but working with a few partners to

40:17.430 --> 40:22.330
start to explore options for

40:23.290 --> 40:26.530
I almost said a much cooler, that's not the

40:27.030 --> 40:31.210
word I should use, a much more

40:31.710 --> 40:35.130
modern opportunity to expand what we're currently doing.

40:35.630 --> 40:38.810
Thank you. I knew Sunnyside would be on the cutting edge.

40:39.050 --> 40:42.330
Councillor Schatz. Thank you. Thank you, Dr. Mathers, and congratulations on

40:42.830 --> 40:46.370
your award. Thank you.

40:46.870 --> 40:50.890
Really appreciate the update on the Sunnyside Home. The work you do there is

40:51.390 --> 40:54.250
amazing and I think very forward thinking. I'm not sure if my question is for

40:54.750 --> 40:58.450
you or for Julie or for

40:58.950 --> 41:02.290
Dr. Wong, but I'll ask it and you can

41:02.790 --> 41:06.130
decide who should answer it. I know that there are other long-term care facilities

41:06.930 --> 41:10.410
that do good research work as well. And of course there are some that don't.

41:10.910 --> 41:14.970
So I wonder what is our role at

41:15.470 --> 41:18.810
Sunnyside in terms of interacting with

41:19.310 --> 41:23.690
other long-term care facilities in the region and how do we kind

41:24.190 --> 41:26.450
of support each other and learn from each other?

41:32.620 --> 41:36.300
I'll let Julie answer that in greater detail. I told you about the Traumatic Informed

41:36.800 --> 41:41.020
Project that we're doing, and it's

41:41.520 --> 41:44.700
done in collaboration with other homes, two other

41:45.200 --> 41:49.460
homes, in this case, the Hillo Lodge in

41:49.960 --> 41:56.660
Ottawa and St.

41:57.160 --> 42:00.260
Joseph's Long-Term Care in Guelph.

42:00.760 --> 42:05.020
So we're definitely working together. I love the question, so thank you

42:05.520 --> 42:08.840
for asking it. From my perspective and I think from the

42:09.340 --> 42:12.250
perspective of our team, we feel very strongly that as a municipal long-term care home,

42:12.750 --> 42:16.200
we have a responsibility to share and spread the opportunities

42:16.700 --> 42:21.040
and the learnings that we have within Sunnyside. The Living Classroom is

42:21.540 --> 42:24.920
actually a beautiful example of that, where we really

42:25.420 --> 42:29.080
saw the opportunity to partner with the Sunnyside Foundation to create a space

42:29.580 --> 42:33.440
where people can come and learn about person-centered care, work alongside our residents, and learn

42:33.940 --> 42:37.560
with them and from them, and then work wherever they choose in the health

42:37.880 --> 42:42.040
system. We obviously hope that people choose Sunnyside,

42:42.540 --> 42:46.280
but honestly, the goal is to create better learning

42:46.360 --> 42:49.960
opportunities so that we can inspire more and

42:50.460 --> 42:54.680
more people to choose healthcare. That commitment extends to

42:55.180 --> 42:59.130
a number of different things, We have about 200 students that we accept

42:59.630 --> 43:02.610
annually in every discipline that you can imagine.

43:03.250 --> 43:07.090
We also take a very active

43:07.330 --> 43:10.850
role with Advantage Association, which is a partnership of municipal

43:11.570 --> 43:15.490
and not for profit homes, and encourage our team to be leaders in

43:16.290 --> 43:19.650
those opportunities, share what we're learning,

43:20.150 --> 43:24.050
share our resources and our policies where appropriate, and learn from others because the

43:24.750 --> 43:28.390
the opportunity to do better in healthcare is always present.

43:28.890 --> 43:32.630
Can I follow up on that? Thank you for that. And I

43:33.130 --> 43:37.470
appreciate that we're working with other municipal

43:37.970 --> 43:41.470
long-term care facilities. But I'm thinking of also our private homes here.

43:41.630 --> 43:45.230
Do we have any role to play there? I know, for example,

43:45.310 --> 43:48.710
I've toured the Schlegel Research Institute

43:49.210 --> 43:52.400
and they have a similar learning centre and

43:52.900 --> 43:56.040
have had for a number of years. So how do we work together to share

43:56.540 --> 43:59.720
information and share learnings? Yes,

44:00.220 --> 44:03.760
very much. And I think that each aspect of the long-term care

44:04.260 --> 44:08.360
sector brings a contribution that's valuable. For myself personally,

44:08.440 --> 44:11.800
I co-chair the long-term care advisory table,

44:12.300 --> 44:15.880
which is made up of a variety of long-term care representatives.

44:16.630 --> 44:19.670
From all aspects of the sector,

44:20.170 --> 44:23.710
as well as a local long-term care

44:24.210 --> 44:27.510
network. I see this as a really important role of

44:28.010 --> 44:31.830
myself and other members of our leaders on

44:32.069 --> 44:35.710
our team to be at those tables and to share

44:36.210 --> 44:39.070
experience, but also to understand the perspective of the private sector and what experiences they're

44:39.570 --> 44:43.456
having and what can we learn from that part of the health

44:43.956 --> 44:47.910
sector. It's truthfully a very challenging service

44:48.410 --> 44:52.550
to deliver on. And some of the questions

44:53.030 --> 44:55.910
today, I think, really pointed to that,

44:57.430 --> 45:00.950
that the regulations that we operate under are very, very restrictive.

45:01.030 --> 45:06.150
And what we need

45:06.550 --> 45:11.750
is creativity and innovation and leaders

45:12.250 --> 45:15.110
sitting at every table possible to learn and make the system better.

45:15.890 --> 45:19.570
I don't see any other questions. Thank you for both for attending

45:20.070 --> 45:23.290
and for the work that you're doing at Sunnyside. I think, Dr. Mayers, you're probably

45:23.790 --> 45:29.130
the most senior person in this room right now who have

45:29.630 --> 45:33.410
been coming back here reporting on a regular basis. So thank

45:33.910 --> 45:37.410
you for being here again. Okay, my pleasure.

45:37.910 --> 45:40.850
Thank you. Item 6.2, the Food Safety Program.

45:41.010 --> 45:44.850
And, oh, excuse me. So that report on Sunnyside Home was

45:45.350 --> 45:48.790
for information purposes only,

45:49.290 --> 45:52.390
no action necessary. Item 6.2.1, the Food Safety Program, and I'll call on Dr.

45:52.890 --> 45:55.790
Wong, our Medical Officer of Health, to introduce the report. And this one is for

45:56.290 --> 45:59.710
information as well. Thank you, Chair, members of council,

46:00.210 --> 46:03.710
for the opportunity to provide just some overview remarks. So today, Public Health is

46:03.950 --> 46:07.470
providing members of regional council

46:07.970 --> 46:12.510
as the Board of Health with the annual update on

46:12.590 --> 46:13.550
its food safety program.

46:14.460 --> 46:18.300
Food safety is

46:19.180 --> 46:22.940
a core public health service and it's also one

46:23.440 --> 46:27.100
of the most visible ways residents experience public health in their

46:27.180 --> 46:31.220
everyday lives. This report outlines key program activities

46:31.720 --> 46:36.380
in 2025 which ensure compliance with provincial food

46:36.880 --> 46:40.420
safety regulations and the Ontario public health standards.

46:40.920 --> 46:42.540
This includes routine inspections of food premises,

46:43.510 --> 46:46.710
investigation of complaints,

46:47.430 --> 46:50.470
review of special events,

46:50.710 --> 46:52.790
and facilitation of licensing requests.

46:54.550 --> 46:57.830
Waterloo Region has in recent years experienced

46:59.030 --> 47:03.070
strong population growth resulting in

47:03.570 --> 47:06.910
an increase in the number and complexity of food premises, inspections,

47:07.410 --> 47:10.430
investigations, and requests for service. In addition to routine inspections of food premises,

47:10.660 --> 47:15.540
Growth has also led to

47:16.040 --> 47:19.620
public health inspectors managing an increase in volume of

47:19.780 --> 47:23.220
work in other program areas, such as rabies

47:23.720 --> 47:27.340
investigations, outbreak management, and other unscheduled priority demands. As a result, public health continues

47:27.840 --> 47:31.140
to implement a risk-based approach

47:31.640 --> 47:35.660
to prioritize inspections and reduce potential health impacts on

47:36.160 --> 47:39.940
the community. Public Health is grateful for Council's

47:40.420 --> 47:44.100
support in adding two Public Health

47:44.600 --> 47:48.060
Inspector positions in 2025. These positions supported Public Health to maintain

47:48.560 --> 47:52.460
high levels of service in a growing food safety

47:52.960 --> 47:56.940
program while managing increased demands across all program areas and supporting Public Health's local response

47:57.440 --> 48:00.860
as well to the

48:01.360 --> 48:05.340
provincial measles outbreak, for which four public health inspectors

48:05.420 --> 48:07.780
were temporarily redeployed in 2025. Thank you. We also have Director Aldo Franco and Associate

48:08.280 --> 48:11.820
Medical Officer of Health, Dr. Rabiya Bana here in

48:12.320 --> 48:14.620
case there are questions. Thank you,

48:15.660 --> 48:19.460
Dr. Wong. Councillor McCabe. Thank you very much for

48:19.960 --> 48:23.566
the report and for the update and for all the work that happens

48:24.066 --> 48:29.920
through this program to keep people safe. I'm just curious about when or

48:30.420 --> 48:33.813
how often forms and things that business owners have to complete to apply for,

48:34.313 --> 48:36.960
you know, licensing and, you know, the regular checks and things like that. Like when

48:37.460 --> 48:41.560
are those forms looked at and reviewed and do you include business

48:42.060 --> 48:47.760
owners in that? I'm just thinking of, you know, business owners particularly

48:48.260 --> 48:51.640
in, you know, in restaurants and coffee shops and things like that. They're,

48:51.880 --> 48:53.040
you know, they are the small business owners who are very, very pressed for time

48:53.540 --> 48:59.170
and their margins are really thin. Just thinking of like how frequently

48:59.670 --> 49:01.730
do your staff meet with some of the, or do you meet with them to

49:02.230 --> 49:06.410
streamline forms, kind of alleviate any kind of bottlenecks, et cetera. Thanks for

49:06.910 --> 49:11.090
providing a bit of information around that. Thank you

49:11.410 --> 49:15.070
for the question through chair. Actually, business licensing goes through the

49:15.570 --> 49:19.170
local municipalities. We don't, oh my God. We get requested for

49:19.730 --> 49:23.250
inspection. Sorry, right? But on the food inspection side. From the

49:23.750 --> 49:27.980
food inspection side, Anyone who wants to

49:28.480 --> 49:30.620
operate or begin an operation,

49:31.120 --> 49:34.500
they should be informing us

49:35.000 --> 49:37.900
of their intent to operate. So we have all that information up on our webpage

49:38.400 --> 49:41.900
and it's an e-form that they can fill out and if they want assistance

49:42.400 --> 49:45.740
in using another way to inform us, they can just call in as

49:46.240 --> 49:47.660
well. And we would review it. That's part of our, one of our, some of

49:48.160 --> 49:52.840
our requests for service. Is providing

49:53.340 --> 49:55.480
that assistance to whether to consulting when someone wants to open up a new business

49:55.960 --> 49:57.590
or to inform them of what they need to do and how we can help

49:58.090 --> 50:01.160
them support their new business.

50:01.800 --> 50:05.280
Thanks for that. And maybe I apologise, I maybe wasn't clear.

50:05.780 --> 50:09.480
I'm just the forms and the processes that you

50:09.980 --> 50:13.480
use for, you know, food safety, whether it's for an event or for the restaurant

50:13.980 --> 50:16.570
owner as they're setting up, et cetera. If you sit down with a group of

50:17.070 --> 50:22.010
them from across the region at any yearly, to take

50:22.510 --> 50:25.930
a look at the processes, just to see where

50:26.430 --> 50:30.770
