01:08:19.230 --> 01:08:22.690
plan. All of our metrics have increased. Now, there's a couple

01:08:22.930 --> 01:08:26.970
of reasons for that. The most important metrics are

01:08:27.470 --> 01:08:30.090
on sudden cardiac arrest, CTAS 1 and CTAS 2 patients, because they are your sickest

01:08:30.590 --> 01:08:34.120
or highest acuity patients. In order to prioritize those and improve those, we increased

01:08:34.680 --> 01:08:37.920
the compliance targets for our lower acuity patients. And I mentioned

01:08:38.420 --> 01:08:41.960
that to you when I asked for a change, the response time performance

01:08:42.460 --> 01:08:46.360
plan from council, because we want to make sure we're prioritizing the sickest patients

01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:50.720
first. So if we can go to the next slide, please. So the

01:08:51.220 --> 01:08:54.680
first bullet is my explanation about for C-test

01:08:55.180 --> 01:08:58.480
3, 4, and 5 calls, a few years ago,

01:08:58.980 --> 01:09:02.610
we were targeting like 12 minute response times. And those are patients

01:09:03.110 --> 01:09:06.530
who can safely wait. If they've been properly triaged at the dispatch centre,

01:09:07.030 --> 01:09:10.210
they can safely wait for longer than that.

01:09:10.710 --> 01:09:14.090
And the theory and the evidence shows that if we ask those patients to wait

01:09:14.590 --> 01:09:18.330
or the system has those patient wait, those patients wait,

01:09:18.830 --> 01:09:22.410
it means when the sudden cardiac arrest call comes in or the CTAS 1

01:09:22.910 --> 01:09:25.570
chest pain stroke patient comes in for a call, we will have a resource that

01:09:26.070 --> 01:09:29.700
can respond to it. And so it's about protecting our resources for your sickest patients.

01:09:30.249 --> 01:09:34.729
And, you know, there's still tons of system pressure

01:09:35.229 --> 01:09:38.329
and there's still room for improvement, but despite that, you know,

01:09:38.809 --> 01:09:42.289
I'm really proud of our team. We, I think, deliver really excellent

01:09:42.789 --> 01:09:45.769
patient care. Our staff work on really, really difficult circumstances,

01:09:45.929 --> 01:09:49.769
which I've reported on before, and they do so with

01:09:50.269 --> 01:09:53.889
a smile on their face and compassion

01:09:54.389 --> 01:09:57.409
in their heart and really, really deliver strongly.

01:09:57.909 --> 01:10:01.220
So we're really pleased about that. Next slide, please. This is where

01:10:01.720 --> 01:10:04.820
we've seen a real big improvement regarding sudden cardiac arrest.

01:10:05.320 --> 01:10:07.900
You can see that the Ministry of Health's target is 50% of the time a

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:11.660
defibrillator is to arrive on scene for a sudden cardiac arrest in six minutes or

01:10:12.160 --> 01:10:16.180
less. We have a regional target to achieve that six minutes or less,

01:10:16.680 --> 01:10:20.220
70% of the time. And in

01:10:20.460 --> 01:10:23.580
2025, we hit that target, and it was the first year we'd

01:10:24.080 --> 01:10:27.040
hit that target in a few years. So again, a demonstration of improvement. So thank

01:10:27.540 --> 01:10:29.600
you. Thank you for your clapping. I appreciate that.

01:10:30.640 --> 01:10:34.240
If you can go to the next slide,

01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:39.320
please. And the rest of these

01:10:39.820 --> 01:10:42.920
are just showing you the improvement from 24 to 2025.

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:46.840
Again, if I can draw your attention to CTAS 3, 4, and 5,

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:51.120
the reason you've seen such a marked improvement in

01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:55.410
the percentages, for example, CTAS 3 from 80% to 92.3 is because as we lengthened

01:10:55.910 --> 01:10:59.570
the time target. So that's the biggest reason you've seen that improvement. So it

01:11:00.070 --> 01:11:03.490
was a data change. Next slide, please. I often get asked about what are the

01:11:03.990 --> 01:11:07.250
strategies we're continuing to do to mitigate challenges within

01:11:07.750 --> 01:11:11.090
the system. Obviously, one that we will continue to do and we meet with

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:15.330
every hospital every two weeks is to continue to

01:11:15.830 --> 01:11:19.370
work on system improvements and patient flow improvements

01:11:19.870 --> 01:11:24.280
for ambulance offload times Again, that has worked for the leadership team

01:11:24.780 --> 01:11:28.440
on our part and our important hospital partners, but it results

01:11:28.840 --> 01:11:32.560
in getting more time back into the system for paramedics out

01:11:33.060 --> 01:11:35.640
on the road responding to calls. We want to utilise and continue to utilise provincial

01:11:36.140 --> 01:11:39.440
funding from the Ministry of Health for the Designated Offload Nurse Program that

01:11:39.940 --> 01:11:42.360
is funded currently at just over $1.5 million a year that allows us, and it's

01:11:42.860 --> 01:11:46.610
100% funded, nothing from the tax levy. And it

01:11:46.770 --> 01:11:49.543
allows us to fund a designated offload nurse position in all three hospitals 24 hours

01:11:50.043 --> 01:11:52.090
a day, seven days a week. And that is again what's helping drive these improvements.

01:11:52.590 --> 01:11:56.156
And we just submitted our proposal for the

01:11:56.656 --> 01:12:00.050
2026-27 provincial fiscal year and hopefully we will see that funding

01:12:00.550 --> 01:12:04.410
increase, if not at least stay the same. We want to continue

01:12:04.910 --> 01:12:08.250
expansion of the patient care model standards that the province is helping

01:12:08.750 --> 01:12:12.770
enable and those are things like treat and

01:12:13.270 --> 01:12:16.590
discharge, treat and refer, and alternate destination. For treatment

01:12:17.090 --> 01:12:21.110
discharge, treatment referral, the goal is that

01:12:21.610 --> 01:12:25.516
it gives paramedics the tool to release a patient from care at the scene rather

01:12:26.016 --> 01:12:28.270
than take them to the hospital. And we're continuing to focus on those efforts.

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:32.070
And then obviously for alternate destination, you've heard us speak and support Heart

01:12:32.570 --> 01:12:36.510
Hub partners at the Horseshoe here about setting up a clinic in the community

01:12:36.990 --> 01:12:40.790
for mental health and addictions patients that we could eventually transport to

01:12:41.290 --> 01:12:43.710
rather than transport them to emerge. And I think we talked about that just a

01:12:44.210 --> 01:12:47.750
couple of months ago, if I'm not mistaken. Also, furthering advancement of community paramedicine program

01:12:48.250 --> 01:12:50.510
to address complex patients that have a high utilization rate of 911, that's important.

01:12:50.750 --> 01:12:54.910
And in May, a report will be coming to the committee regarding our performance

01:12:55.410 --> 01:12:58.790
for the community paramedicine program. So happy

01:12:59.290 --> 01:13:02.830
to report on that in May, but also take questions

01:13:03.330 --> 01:13:06.710
today if necessary. And then the other leap we made was in early

01:13:07.210 --> 01:13:11.540
January, we reassigned operation superintendents into the dispatch centre run

01:13:12.040 --> 01:13:17.180
by the Ministry of Health. We're, I think, the fifth or sixth community across

01:13:17.680 --> 01:13:21.100
the province to do this. What this means is now we have right in the

01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:24.180
dispatch centre on the floor where calls are being taken and resources being dispatched,

01:13:24.680 --> 01:13:26.620
we have a representative in there 12 hours a day, seven days a week,

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:29.540
helping make sure that our deployment plan is being followed accurately, helping support our Ministry

01:13:30.040 --> 01:13:33.990
of Health partners in managing difficult 911 calls, helping make sure our resources are getting

01:13:34.380 --> 01:13:38.060
off work on time, all those types of things.

01:13:38.560 --> 01:13:41.700
And we really are starting in the first couple of months, we've seen

01:13:42.200 --> 01:13:45.460
some gradual improvements, but in terms of the relationship and the

01:13:45.960 --> 01:13:49.820
partnership with the Ministry of Health, we've seen large improvement

01:13:49.899 --> 01:13:53.900
and are hoping that that program will continue. Next slide, please. I think I

01:13:54.400 --> 01:13:57.620
reported to council in the fall that we got

01:13:58.120 --> 01:14:01.580
a new dispatch system on December 4th of last year, so we've now just

01:14:01.930 --> 01:14:05.210
we just hit about three months of that dispatch system called MPDS. And we're excited

01:14:05.710 --> 01:14:07.530
to see how that dispatch system and those changes will also improve the metrics.

01:14:08.030 --> 01:14:12.263
Because the old dispatch system, which I've reported on

01:14:12.763 --> 01:14:16.650
a few times, was fairly old, fairly antiquated, and it was like a

01:14:17.150 --> 01:14:19.850
blunt force instrument in terms of trying to get resources to the right calls.

01:14:19.930 --> 01:14:22.770
And so we were responding 70% of the time, code 4,

01:14:23.270 --> 01:14:26.730
lights and sirens, to calls of all natures.

01:14:28.100 --> 01:14:31.460
And then finding out when we got there that those

01:14:31.960 --> 01:14:35.580
patients weren't actually that sick. When that was

01:14:36.080 --> 01:14:39.860
happening under the old system, it meant that if I was

01:14:40.360 --> 01:14:44.420
being dispatched to a code for abdominal pain for three, the patient had abdominal

01:14:44.920 --> 01:14:48.300
pain for three days, I couldn't be reassigned to a

01:14:48.800 --> 01:14:52.420
code for cardiac arrest. So we weren't utilising our resources effectively. Under the new

01:14:52.920 --> 01:14:56.140
system, MPDS, now the highest acuity calls get a specific specific designation called Code Purple,

01:14:56.640 --> 01:14:57.960
and we can reassign from any of the lower codes to making sure the Code

01:14:58.460 --> 01:14:58.800
Purple gets the quickest and most timely response.

01:15:02.320 --> 01:15:06.080
And so it's gone to that five level system. Our staff,

01:15:07.280 --> 01:15:10.504
I think, have largely embraced it. We've been working with our dispatch partners to making

01:15:11.004 --> 01:15:13.560
sure it's being managed effectively, and we are seeing improvements in that regard. And I

01:15:14.060 --> 01:15:18.240
hope to report a year from now that that

01:15:18.320 --> 01:15:21.690
improvement has been really significant. We have changed how our

01:15:22.190 --> 01:15:25.890
emergency response units are being utilised. We have now staffed them with advanced care paramedics.

01:15:26.390 --> 01:15:29.730
Previously, they were with primary care paramedics. And the

01:15:30.230 --> 01:15:35.090
prevailing evidence and theory on that is the advanced care

01:15:35.590 --> 01:15:37.482
paramedics, we don't have a lot of them in the system. We roughly have 45

01:15:37.982 --> 01:15:40.260
to 50 out of all of our staff are advanced care paramedics. So when only

01:15:40.760 --> 01:15:41.263
four or five are on duty in a day, we want to make sure we

01:15:41.763 --> 01:15:45.170
get them to the highest acuity patients as often as possible because

01:15:45.670 --> 01:15:49.420
that's where they have their most impact. So by putting them in response

01:15:49.920 --> 01:15:51.980
units, that allows them to zoom around and attach themselves and get dispatched to those

01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:55.820
patients as much as possible. The other thing we've

01:15:56.320 --> 01:15:58.020
done is move them primarily the response zones are in the central core of the

01:15:58.520 --> 01:16:02.740
region. Previously, as council

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:06.740
knows, they were assigned to

01:16:07.240 --> 01:16:10.940
a post in rural townships.

01:16:11.440 --> 01:16:14.780
The reason we've done this is by putting response units in the

01:16:15.280 --> 01:16:18.780
urban core, and hopefully they and what we are seeing is them responding to calls

01:16:19.280 --> 01:16:23.220
and then cancelling off ambulances altogether because a

01:16:23.720 --> 01:16:27.540
single paramedic was able to manage the call from

01:16:27.700 --> 01:16:32.820
start to finish. What we've been able to do with all this other resource availability

01:16:33.700 --> 01:16:36.900
is reprioritize rural areas to actually have ambulance coverage, not just response unit coverage.

01:16:37.400 --> 01:16:37.882
And one of the advantages to that is a single resource managing a call in

01:16:38.382 --> 01:16:42.340
a rural Township instead of the response unit and the ambulance,

01:16:42.840 --> 01:16:46.070
which is obviously a

01:16:46.570 --> 01:16:50.430
little less cost effective. We also wanted part of the master plan that

01:16:50.930 --> 01:16:53.070
you approved in September includes a recommendation to develop and enhance a clinical response strategy

01:16:53.570 --> 01:16:56.790
for all areas of the region. So this change to response units and how we

01:16:57.290 --> 01:17:00.270
utilise advanced care paramedics is part of that. And we want to make sure,

01:17:00.430 --> 01:17:03.670
as I mentioned, that we're prioritizing ambulance coverage for those

01:17:04.170 --> 01:17:07.470
communities and then making sure, as I already mentioned, advanced care

01:17:07.970 --> 01:17:11.480
paramedics are getting to the high security

01:17:11.980 --> 01:17:13.760
patients as often as possible. That's why they took the training is to be able

01:17:14.260 --> 01:17:18.240
to make the most impact on our sickest patients. The other thing that we

01:17:18.740 --> 01:17:20.920
take a lot of pride in is that we don't make these decisions on a

01:17:21.420 --> 01:17:26.000
whim. We make these decisions based on data. And so again,

01:17:26.500 --> 01:17:28.760
with the support of our EHA team, we have dashboards internally that we use to

01:17:29.260 --> 01:17:33.040
adjust deployment plans accordingly. And if we see data trending in the wrong

01:17:33.540 --> 01:17:37.330
direction, we'll make a different decision. If we see it continuing in the

01:17:37.830 --> 01:17:40.930
direction we want it to go in, then we know we've made

01:17:41.430 --> 01:17:46.050
a good decision and we can make those timely decisions more

01:17:46.550 --> 01:17:49.810
regularly. And then the final plug, I guess, is the next

01:17:50.310 --> 01:17:51.330
thing we need is a mitigation strategy as council support to continue to implement the

01:17:51.830 --> 01:17:55.530
recommendations in the master plan that

01:17:56.030 --> 01:17:59.970
were approved in September. If I may remind council,

01:18:02.040 --> 01:18:04.600
That plan indicated that we would have a 60% increase in 911 call volume by

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:08.640
the year 2035. So as we see those call volumes increase, we need to

01:18:09.140 --> 01:18:12.920
make sure right now that we believe the service is right size to manage

01:18:13.420 --> 01:18:17.240
demand, but we're going to need to continue to enhance service

01:18:17.400 --> 01:18:21.160
to manage that demand, assuming, I think, that we all want

01:18:21.800 --> 01:18:25.320
response times to be positive in terms of improvement, and we

01:18:25.820 --> 01:18:28.370
all want patients to get the care they need when they need it. So that

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:32.210
is all I have and I'm happy to take

01:18:32.710 --> 01:18:36.770
any questions. Thank you, Chief. We do have quite a few

01:18:37.270 --> 01:18:39.610
questions. Councillor Schantz. Thank you.

01:18:40.110 --> 01:18:43.370
And thank you, Chief. I know

01:18:43.870 --> 01:18:47.810
that your day wouldn't be complete unless I commented on

01:18:48.310 --> 01:18:51.890
offload delays and I'm so, so glad to see them

01:18:52.390 --> 01:18:57.940
drop as they have and also to To just make the point that,

01:18:58.440 --> 01:19:02.660
you know, when we get things under control, it can be

01:19:03.160 --> 01:19:07.820
expensive and difficult to get there. But in the end,

01:19:08.320 --> 01:19:12.140
the investment in the total system is well

01:19:12.640 --> 01:19:15.700
worth it. And I think that's something that we as a council

01:19:16.200 --> 01:19:19.740
need to remember when we're advocating as well to the province that, you know,

01:19:20.240 --> 01:19:24.240
we might not we do

01:19:24.740 --> 01:19:28.360
not see all of the

01:19:28.860 --> 01:19:34.720
benefits here, but the system as

01:19:34.880 --> 01:19:38.680
a whole will see benefits and hopefully we can get a little better

01:19:39.180 --> 01:19:42.440
distribution of funds accordingly. Thank you. Councillor Wolfe.

01:19:42.940 --> 01:19:46.120
Through you, Mr. Chair. Thank you, Chief.

01:19:46.620 --> 01:19:50.680
Great report. Congratulations on providing excellent patient care and excellent response times.

01:19:51.180 --> 01:19:53.320
I think you've improved in every area.

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:58.000
Under offload delay hours, is the

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:02.080
Cambridge Hospital still your greatest number of hours? And if

01:20:02.580 --> 01:20:05.640
so, why do you think that is? Through you, Chair. Yes, unfortunately they are at

01:20:06.140 --> 01:20:10.280
this time, although it's important to acknowledge that

01:20:10.780 --> 01:20:14.880
all three emerg sites have greatly improved. I think that Cambridge has some unique

01:20:15.490 --> 01:20:18.970
challenges that which are similar to our challenges in supporting the

01:20:19.470 --> 01:20:22.610
needs of the Cambridge community. And one of the biggest ones is

01:20:23.110 --> 01:20:27.010
they're the only hospital in town for south of the 401.

01:20:27.570 --> 01:20:30.810
So they tend to be a

01:20:31.310 --> 01:20:35.250
catch all for patients of all types and all acuity, whereas for Ren

01:20:35.750 --> 01:20:38.530
they have two facilities currently that are fairly close together that can support each other

01:20:39.030 --> 01:20:42.660
in that regard. All that being said, I think that

01:20:43.160 --> 01:20:46.260
Cambridge is on an improvement journey.

01:20:46.760 --> 01:20:49.500
In fact, I know they are because we meet with them regularly and their executives

01:20:50.000 --> 01:20:52.220
say we don't want to be third, we want to be first in terms of

01:20:52.720 --> 01:20:55.980
this metric. So some healthy competition amongst the hospital

01:20:56.480 --> 01:20:58.580
sites to drive improvement with each other is really helpful. And I know that they're

01:20:59.080 --> 01:21:02.820
working with Ren to say, hey, what's working in your

01:21:03.320 --> 01:21:06.220
department? Maybe we can bring it to our department.

01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:12.800
But it is, Currently they are

01:21:13.300 --> 01:21:16.840
the highest 90th percentile all offload delays for us,

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:21.040
yes. And I just wanted to comment on attending,

01:21:21.540 --> 01:21:26.200
I think, your shield ceremony for new recruits. How impressed

01:21:26.520 --> 01:21:30.040
I was by the people going into become

01:21:30.540 --> 01:21:34.360
paramedics and by their diversity and the range of backgrounds. So looking at that

01:21:34.860 --> 01:21:38.980
recruit class. I know we're going to

01:21:39.480 --> 01:21:42.820
be in good hands in the future. Thank you. Through you, Mr. Chair,

01:21:43.320 --> 01:21:47.260
I really appreciate your comments on that regard. We're really proud of our recruitment

01:21:47.760 --> 01:21:51.620
strategies. And just a Shameless plug. You'll be getting an

01:21:52.120 --> 01:21:55.660
invitation to another epaulette ceremony for early June shortly. Okay. Thank you. No problem.

01:21:56.160 --> 01:21:59.020
Thank you. Thank you, chair. Thank you,

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:02.020
Chief, just for coming and presenting this very encouraging report.

01:22:02.950 --> 01:22:06.430
I know there was more to your report

01:22:06.930 --> 01:22:10.350
than offload delays, but the improvement, vast improvement is very much encouraging. So I

01:22:10.850 --> 01:22:14.630
just want to follow up on Councillor Schantz's comments around how did

01:22:15.110 --> 01:22:18.429
we get such a vast reduction in offload

01:22:18.929 --> 01:22:21.430
delays and how do we ensure that we maintain that,

01:22:22.230 --> 01:22:25.430
knowing that it's best for patients

01:22:25.510 --> 01:22:27.670
and it's best for first responders? Thank you, Mr. Chair. That's an excellent question.

01:22:28.240 --> 01:22:33.640
I think the answer to what got unlocked was really about collaboration and

01:22:34.140 --> 01:22:37.480
partnership between our leadership team here, some of whom are in the audience, and hospital

01:22:37.980 --> 01:22:42.800
leaders, both at the manager and director level in the emergency rooms,

01:22:43.040 --> 01:22:46.200
but also senior vice presidents and CEOs at the

01:22:46.700 --> 01:22:50.400
hospitals. I've done a lot of work across the province for

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:53.880
offload delays through the Ontario Association of Paramedic Chiefs and

01:22:54.380 --> 01:22:57.830
the one enabler that we think is the most important is when senior

01:22:58.330 --> 01:23:01.870
hospital leadership says offload delays are not acceptable in my facility.

01:23:02.370 --> 01:23:03.990
Because if it starts at the top, staff will follow. And I've said to my

01:23:04.490 --> 01:23:07.790
staff, you know, how do you expect a charge nurse who's really busy on

01:23:08.290 --> 01:23:11.750
a Saturday night to worry about ambulance

01:23:12.250 --> 01:23:14.110
offload times if it's not a priority for the whole hospital on weekdays, for example.

01:23:14.610 --> 01:23:19.790
And so I think the biggest enabler, to answer your question, Councillor Hunnik,

01:23:20.290 --> 01:23:24.600
is that that we have senior leadership buy

01:23:25.100 --> 01:23:28.600
in from the hospitals and they've made it a whole hospital

01:23:29.100 --> 01:23:31.760
metric. In fact, ambulance offload time is a metric now that every ward in the

01:23:32.260 --> 01:23:36.120
hospital sees it at all three sites.

01:23:36.200 --> 01:23:39.560
So a med surg ward sees it, the mental

01:23:40.060 --> 01:23:42.600
health ward sees it, every ward sees it. So they've all created buy in for

01:23:43.100 --> 01:23:47.080
it because again, it's about patient flow.

01:23:47.580 --> 01:23:48.800
We bring them patients. As you all know, you've all used the emergency department.

01:23:49.300 --> 01:23:53.090
Patients go to the emergency department by taxi, by private means. And it's where

01:23:53.810 --> 01:23:58.010
can they, once those patients have been assessed and stabilized

01:23:58.510 --> 01:24:00.650
and emerged, where do they go from there? And so the hospitals have done,

01:24:01.150 --> 01:24:03.810
I think, a really, really bang up job of improving systems in that regard.

01:24:04.930 --> 01:24:07.644
And I think the other enabler is that Ontario Health and the Ministry of Health

01:24:08.144 --> 01:24:10.850
have made this a priority. Every three to four months, I meet with senior executives

01:24:11.350 --> 01:24:15.130
of the hospital, the assistant deputy minister, the Ontario Health deputy minister,

01:24:15.630 --> 01:24:18.620
and they hold us to task to our metrics and say, okay, the provincial expectation

01:24:19.180 --> 01:24:22.980
for ambulance offload time is 30 minutes.

01:24:23.480 --> 01:24:27.540
Why are you at 40 minutes? Why are you at 60

01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:31.620
minutes? Why are you at 20 minutes? That type of thing. So their

01:24:32.120 --> 01:24:34.180
advocacy and pushing the hospitals to improve has been very helpful as well. So just

01:24:34.680 --> 01:24:38.460
to make sure I'm following you correctly, the fear of the rebound offload delays is

01:24:38.960 --> 01:24:42.420
lessening because it's a priority for the entire

01:24:42.970 --> 01:24:45.930
system. Yes. Yes, I think you, Mr. Chair, I would agree with that. I do

01:24:46.430 --> 01:24:50.170
say we do see inflections in the data. So, for example,

01:24:50.670 --> 01:24:54.090
I've mentioned this before, December and January tends to be respiratory season in the health

01:24:54.590 --> 01:24:58.210
care system. So we do see an increase in ambulance

01:24:58.710 --> 01:25:02.090
offload times for those months. Yeah. And we know to expect it.

01:25:02.590 --> 01:25:04.810
But, but if we can keep ambulance offload times the other months to, you know,

01:25:05.310 --> 01:25:08.930
below 30 minutes, when it jumps up to 35 minutes or 40 minutes in

01:25:09.430 --> 01:25:11.890
those months, we can actually hold we can manage during those times. You know,

01:25:12.390 --> 01:25:15.890
not a couple of years ago, the ambulance offload

01:25:16.390 --> 01:25:19.810
time in the 90th percentile was over two hours. So it's really,

01:25:20.310 --> 01:25:23.850
really significant improvements. Thank you. Thank you. Councillor Rodriguez.

01:25:24.350 --> 01:25:27.130
Thank you, Chair and Chief. Thanks for a great, a great news story this morning

01:25:27.630 --> 01:25:31.170
and thanks to the team

01:25:31.670 --> 01:25:34.250
that's here and on the road for helping us get there. Two questions for you

01:25:34.750 --> 01:25:38.700
on the master plans. We've approved a plan Could you comment on when,

01:25:39.200 --> 01:25:42.060
you know, is it through the annual budget cycle that you expect to be coming

01:25:42.560 --> 01:25:46.420
forward with your next year strategy or is there, you know, some type of

01:25:46.920 --> 01:25:50.140
report that comes leading up to budget to help sort of begin those conversations?

01:25:50.500 --> 01:25:54.260
I think to your point,

01:25:54.760 --> 01:25:58.020
we need to not let, you know,

01:25:58.520 --> 01:26:00.980
our foot off the gas and to keep understanding what the incremental steps need to

01:26:01.480 --> 01:26:04.640
be. So if you could comment on that. Thank you, Mr. Chair.

01:26:05.140 --> 01:26:08.880
I really love the question. For us, the master plan and all the

01:26:09.380 --> 01:26:11.680
recommendations in it is our guiding document for the next 10 years. Some of those

01:26:12.180 --> 01:26:15.840
recommendations do not require financial resources, such as developing a clinical response strategy.

01:26:16.340 --> 01:26:20.680
That's something that's the responsibility of myself and my

01:26:21.180 --> 01:26:25.720
team. Coming up with other strategies that are more

01:26:26.220 --> 01:26:29.320
system improvement strategies than they are resource improvement strategies. However,

01:26:29.820 --> 01:26:33.880
in order to manage call volumes and

01:26:34.380 --> 01:26:40.240
other things, that incremental improvement will be

01:26:40.740 --> 01:26:45.040
required. The general rule is we will use the master plan

01:26:45.540 --> 01:26:49.160
to inform our budget asks. That's the general rule. As a quick example,

01:26:49.660 --> 01:26:53.200
the master plan did call for in 2026 an

01:26:53.700 --> 01:26:56.150
expansion of our rapid response unit program. But unfortunately, due to the fiscal pressures.

01:26:56.650 --> 01:26:58.990
We were not able to increase FTEs and stuff like that for this year.

01:26:59.490 --> 01:27:03.430
So that's why it didn't go through. However, you can expect

01:27:03.930 --> 01:27:07.430
that I'll probably be putting it in for the 2027 budget,

01:27:07.750 --> 01:27:11.030
if that's okay. So, no, that's great. And, and appreciate that. I think that's,

01:27:11.530 --> 01:27:16.590
it's great to know that there are those things

01:27:17.090 --> 01:27:19.030
that are, you know, lower, no cost and the bigger asks, similar to Councillor Shantz.

01:27:19.530 --> 01:27:22.190
I think it wouldn't be a conversation with you if I, I didn't pick up

01:27:22.690 --> 01:27:28.410
on our budget. Conversation around community paramedicine. I think it is such

01:27:28.910 --> 01:27:32.650
a great program and there's some

01:27:32.810 --> 01:27:36.730
promising news, I think, from the province that this

01:27:37.230 --> 01:27:40.850
is a fully funded program and that you

01:27:41.350 --> 01:27:44.210
said you'd be coming back in May.

01:27:44.710 --> 01:27:47.770
Can you speak to the status of that program, what we know now, sort of

01:27:48.270 --> 01:27:50.570
a few months out of budget? Because I think this is a program we need

01:27:50.810 --> 01:27:54.170
to keep in the spotlight. Yeah, and through you,

01:27:54.670 --> 01:27:58.210
Mr. Chair, thank you for the question. You know,

01:27:58.710 --> 01:28:02.010
our overall strategy right now, just to remind

01:28:02.490 --> 01:28:05.690
council, we're about 90% traditional 9-1-1 response and 10%

01:28:05.930 --> 01:28:09.410
community paramedicine. And the way of the future for paramedic services is

01:28:09.910 --> 01:28:13.010
to change that ratio so that you have,

01:28:13.510 --> 01:28:17.570
you know, 25-30% community paramedicine and 70% 9-1-1 responses because the goal

01:28:18.070 --> 01:28:22.130
of community paramedicine programs is to support patients earlier in their needs so they

01:28:22.630 --> 01:28:25.970
don't need to call 9-1-1 at all. That's kind of the

01:28:26.530 --> 01:28:29.890
primary goal. I can tell you that, save and

01:28:30.390 --> 01:28:30.943
except for one position, we're fully staffed in our CP program now. And we are,

01:28:31.443 --> 01:28:33.298
to your point, fully funded. The Ministry of Long-Term Care announced that CP LTC funding

01:28:33.798 --> 01:28:37.530
or Community Paramedicine Long-Term Care

01:28:38.030 --> 01:28:39.970
funding, as it's called,

01:28:41.170 --> 01:28:44.530
was going to be base funded instead of annual grant funding,

01:28:45.030 --> 01:28:48.310
which is a real big kind of milestone for us. That announcement was made

01:28:48.810 --> 01:28:52.150
in August. That's supposed to take effect

01:28:52.650 --> 01:28:56.070
April 1st of this year, but we have not received

01:28:56.570 --> 01:28:59.990
the funding letter yet, which is also not entirely surprising.

01:29:00.490 --> 01:29:04.510
That is sometimes the way the province works, as many people

01:29:04.830 --> 01:29:06.430
around the horseshoe knows. But,

01:29:07.870 --> 01:29:09.062
you know, it's my sincere hope that once we get the funding letter, it'll be

01:29:09.562 --> 01:29:12.750
the same as what we previously received or increased.

01:29:13.400 --> 01:29:17.240
Reduced. And then over time, we'll use again

01:29:17.560 --> 01:29:20.920
data to inform metrics to say, hey,

01:29:21.080 --> 01:29:25.120
you need to continue to invest in us at a greater rate.

01:29:25.620 --> 01:29:29.359
And as a reminder for councillors, that's 100% funded by the province through two ministries,

01:29:29.859 --> 01:29:33.280
both long-term care at about $3 million a year, and then Ontario Health through $300,000

01:29:33.780 --> 01:29:38.360
a year. And it's a huge value for the dollar because

01:29:38.860 --> 01:29:42.890
if we can get community paramedics supporting people in their

01:29:43.390 --> 01:29:47.090
places where they live with complex medical needs and they don't need to call

01:29:47.590 --> 01:29:50.770
911, it actually saves the system money because they're not going to the hospital and

01:29:51.270 --> 01:29:53.930
all those types of things. I think that there is room for growth in that

01:29:54.430 --> 01:29:57.850
program that we certainly want to see and we've had conversations

01:29:58.330 --> 01:30:01.810
with Ren about helping them with discharge planning

01:30:02.310 --> 01:30:05.970
for patients who are being discharged from hospital and

01:30:06.470 --> 01:30:10.320
also conversations with Peter and his team in community services and Sunny side

01:30:10.820 --> 01:30:12.600
specifically about how to support those areas.

01:30:14.920 --> 01:30:18.600
And then one of the other things in our

01:30:19.000 --> 01:30:22.440
annual business plan for this year for community paramedicine is

01:30:22.940 --> 01:30:26.160
to provide clinic offerings in regionally owned supportive housing buildings. Because we know that those

01:30:26.660 --> 01:30:28.560
buildings generate a lot of 9-1-1 calls, so rent geared to income, those types of

01:30:29.060 --> 01:30:32.480
buildings, social determinants of health. I'm sure council knows all about

01:30:32.980 --> 01:30:36.280
that. We want to put clinics in those places one day a

01:30:36.520 --> 01:30:37.880
week, two days a week,

01:30:38.790 --> 01:30:42.550
one day a month, whatever it is, whatever the building needs, with the

01:30:43.050 --> 01:30:46.530
theory being that if we can provide supportive kind of preventive care, then that building,

01:30:47.030 --> 01:30:49.590
we should see a decrease in 911 calls from that building.

01:30:50.150 --> 01:30:53.909
So we're starting with the regionally owned buildings and then with a

01:30:54.409 --> 01:30:57.990
long-term goal to expand to other not-for-profit housing groups. Thank you.

01:30:58.490 --> 01:31:04.310
I look forward to the May

01:31:04.810 --> 01:31:08.243
report. And if there's anything we can do in the meantime, please let us know.

