01:31:30.200 --> 01:31:33.686
can. I'm going to talk fast. Thank you very much for all you

01:31:34.186 --> 01:31:37.700
do. You did a great job. I was on the

01:31:38.080 --> 01:31:41.560
Master Plan in 2014, and you've

01:31:42.060 --> 01:31:45.520
come a long way. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you, Councillor Foxton, Councillor McCabe.

01:31:46.000 --> 01:31:49.280
Thanks very much through you, Chair, and thanks to the whole team,

01:31:49.520 --> 01:31:54.000
as others have said, for tremendous improvements and

01:31:54.500 --> 01:31:57.440
the data is really, really helpful. So thank you very much. I'm curious,

01:31:58.240 --> 01:32:01.120
and you don't have to answer this right now, but when it said that the,

01:32:01.620 --> 01:32:05.120
about the, I'm curious about how much time and how

01:32:05.850 --> 01:32:09.090
how the time saved translates into staff, you know, where it said

01:32:09.590 --> 01:32:13.850
that it saved three shifts of 12-hour shifts six

01:32:14.350 --> 01:32:17.930
times per day. Did I get that right? Does that mean

01:32:18.430 --> 01:32:22.370
that six paramedics? Anyway, we're looking at slides. Maybe while you're asking a

01:32:22.870 --> 01:32:26.850
question, just got a few slides. I'm just curious

01:32:27.350 --> 01:32:30.530
what that looks like in

01:32:31.030 --> 01:32:34.110
terms of actually staff and impacts there. You can just email us that

01:32:34.610 --> 01:32:38.510
if you'd like, but I just, 153 days, I'm just curious what

01:32:39.010 --> 01:32:42.110
that, or sorry, 453 days, but that actually looks like-- I'll, through you, Mr.

01:32:42.610 --> 01:32:47.790
Chair, I can give a more detailed response via

01:32:48.290 --> 01:32:51.270
an email response if Commissioner Wong's okay with that. Okay,

01:32:51.770 --> 01:32:57.390
thanks. And then the other question I had was, because of the prioritization of

01:32:57.890 --> 01:33:01.450
issues, Are we doing, or your

01:33:01.950 --> 01:33:05.650
team doing some more communications out regularly

01:33:06.150 --> 01:33:10.010
to residents to let them know, you know, if you call

01:33:10.510 --> 01:33:14.610
and it's not an, like you can call, an ambulance

01:33:14.690 --> 01:33:19.410
might be sent, but then maybe changed

01:33:19.910 --> 01:33:22.650
on route because, you know, if you sprained your ankle, a heart attack takes priority.

01:33:23.150 --> 01:33:25.050
Like is there that kind of ongoing communications happening as well so people understand understand

01:33:25.550 --> 01:33:28.330
the priority system that's in place? Great question. And through your chair.

01:33:28.830 --> 01:33:32.650
And I guess part of it is, and maybe it will eventually over time

01:33:33.150 --> 01:33:35.470
help reduce maybe some of the calls we get, because I think some of the

01:33:35.970 --> 01:33:37.250
calls come in like they're not emergencies, right? They're things, yeah.

01:33:38.770 --> 01:33:42.130
So through your Mr. Chair, I'll answer your last point

01:33:42.630 --> 01:33:46.410
first. Yes, we respond of those 66,603 calls we did last year,

01:33:46.910 --> 01:33:50.130
a good portion of them were not, we would not have been deemed medical emergencies.

01:33:50.540 --> 01:33:53.900
Very clearly because they're C-TEST 5 or C-TEST 4 patients.

01:33:54.400 --> 01:33:57.580
I think there's a couple points to that element.

01:33:58.080 --> 01:34:01.420
One is that often people call 911 because

01:34:01.920 --> 01:34:05.060
it's the time of day or it's

01:34:05.560 --> 01:34:08.620
a holiday or whatever it is, and they can't get access to healthcare through another

01:34:09.120 --> 01:34:13.860
means, so they call us. We tend to be a safety net in

01:34:14.360 --> 01:34:17.060
that regard and take that responsibility quite seriously. I think we also need to be

01:34:17.560 --> 01:34:19.676
careful. Yes, we do some social media messaging about these things, But I'll give you

01:34:20.176 --> 01:34:21.566
an example. I was speaking to a colleague from London Ambulance in the UK,

01:34:22.066 --> 01:34:24.450
in the NHS trust there, and they went through on a huge when to call

01:34:24.950 --> 01:34:28.510
999 campaign and immediately saw after

01:34:29.010 --> 01:34:32.170
they finished their campaign an increase in 999 call volume. So you have to be

01:34:32.670 --> 01:34:35.930
really, really mindful about how you position your messaging about

01:34:36.430 --> 01:34:38.040
that. But we, and the last thing I'll say is we did four or five

01:34:38.540 --> 01:34:43.090
public information sessions leading up to

01:34:43.590 --> 01:34:47.070
MPDS being implemented. And I think other than, I think we

01:34:47.570 --> 01:34:49.816
maybe had 12 people attend all four or five. So despite best efforts to put

01:34:50.316 --> 01:34:53.390
ourselves out there and be ready to answer questions from the public, we didn't get

01:34:53.890 --> 01:34:57.230
a lot of uptake in those sessions. Maybe it'll be

01:34:57.730 --> 01:35:01.070
